  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Using renewable energy to improve livelihoods for Kenya&apos;&apos;s fisherfolk</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Kenyan communities on the shore of Lake Victoria are amongst the poorest in East Africa. About 70% of their members live below the poverty line and are heavily reliant on fishing for subsistence and income. Without electricity for chilling or freezing, fish is highly perishable and must be sold or consumed soon after landing. This leaves local fisher-folk with no bargaining power to secure a fair price for their catch. The communities also urgently need to diversify their economic activities in order to reduce business risks and improve nutritional levels. This project will test and disseminate affordable small-scale solutions for renewable energy from wind and solar sources. It will facilitate access to appropriate technology and equipment needed for the fish cold chain and irrigation, and will provide relevant information and training to fishermen, women traders and farmers. It will support community-based structures to manage and maintain joint facilities, and will encourage entrepreneurs to switch to &#x2018;greener&apos; products: for example, kerosene sellers will become suppliers of solar-powered lamps for night fishing and households. As a result, the local population will have more sustainable access to renewable energy. This will significantly enhance income levels and improve nutrition and health.</narrative>
  </description>
